WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.640
"Hello everyone, we are here with Lukas Biewald,&nbsp;
CEO and co-founder of Weights &amp; Biases. I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.200
love to talk to you about your journey. Thank&nbsp;
you for being here; it's really exciting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.200
have you here."
"Totally, yeah."&nbsp;

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:17.200
"So tell me a little bit about how you&nbsp;
got started in AI. Well, actually, let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.120
just start there: how did you get started in AI?"
"Well, it goes way back for me actually. I always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.320
loved to play games, and when I was a pretty&nbsp;
little kid, I was always trying to program my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.600
computer to play games. So I was kind of thinking&nbsp;
about it, I was like, 'How would you make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.480
computer that could beat you at like Connect&nbsp;
4?' And I tried all these different strategies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.440
Then when I got to college, I took a class with a&nbsp;
young professor who was Daphne Koller - actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:46.040
gone on to be really famous - but she taught my&nbsp;
first AI class. We did reinforcement learning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.400
beat Othello, so we spent - I remember like&nbsp;
48 hours in the computer lab. At that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.840
you had to go to the lab to do it. I remember&nbsp;
watching the computer get better and better, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.360
it starts off really bad, and then suddenly it's&nbsp;
like better than me, and then suddenly it's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.480
totally unbeatable. It almost feels like watching&nbsp;
a human learn. It was just this incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.480
visceral experience, and I never went back."
"Totally. Wow, that's amazing. So you started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.280
really young in this interest in&nbsp;
reinforcement learning, kind of."&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.800
"Well, you know, I wasn't doing AI. I was just&nbsp;
literally... but I think actually thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.480
the problem for years really kind of made&nbsp;
it more compelling when I got the solution."&nbsp;

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:28.560
"Totally, like you finally found it. You&nbsp;
finally found your thing. Yeah. How did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.880
you get started creating Weights and Biases?"
"I started creating Weights and Biases because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.880
I really wanted to help people doing AI&nbsp;
engineering. What really happened was I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.400
been running a company called CrowdFlower that did&nbsp;
data labeling for like 10 years, and I had gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:49.600
a little disconnected from - we called it machine&nbsp;
learning at the time. What happened was the space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.040
had kind of taken off. It had gone from running&nbsp;
on CPUs to GPUs, all these things had changed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:02.720
When my first company sold, I thought that maybe&nbsp;
things had gotten easier for doing AI development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.920
but it actually had gotten a lot harder. You&nbsp;
were getting like CUDA errors and weird compiler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.280
errors, and kind of all these issues were coming&nbsp;
up. I really wanted to make better tools for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.000
people that were doing that AI engineering work."
"How did you get started with Weights and Biases?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.200
What were the first tools you made for the&nbsp;
platform, and then how has it evolved over time?"&nbsp;

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:26.360
"Well, it really started from this question of&nbsp;
what do AI engineers actually need. We went to our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.160
friends and we said, 'Hey, would you use these&nbsp;
tools?' And at first, they were like, 'No.'"&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.280
"Good feedback."
"Yeah, good feedback. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:42.280
kind of keep iterating, but the very first thing&nbsp;
that really kind of captured people was experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:47.960
tracking. At the time, it was really unclear&nbsp;
what that was. It wasn't in any VC's diagram or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.000
any company's workflow diagram. I'd always had&nbsp;
this problem, even when I was in grad school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.960
of how do you keep track of all the things&nbsp;
that you tried? It's a pain to go back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:02.400
try to figure out what you did. So that was&nbsp;
kind of like a passion project because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.840
had all experienced that pain. But then people&nbsp;
really liked it, and so it was really exciting."&nbsp;

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.120
"Brilliant. So the experiment tracking, is that&nbsp;
when you're doing training flows and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:15.720
trying different hyperparameter configurations,&nbsp;
and then you're able to track all of that?"&nbsp;

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:19.720
"Yeah, although we kind of designed it broadly.&nbsp;
So in the beginning, it was like, you change a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.280
hyperparameter, but then it's like, okay, what&nbsp;
happens if you change some data? Now it's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.400
what happens if you change a prompt? So there's&nbsp;
lots of different things that you could change."&nbsp;

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.680
"Wow."
"And we try to keep track of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.760
everything that you do and what the results are."
"That's amazing. So Weights and Biases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.840
I have used it for my fine-tuning guides, and&nbsp;
it's been so helpful. All of the people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.840
have used the guides have said, 'Oh my gosh,&nbsp;
it's so great to be able to map the loss curve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:48.280
and see which runs are better, etc.' And so you're&nbsp;
saying you can actually map different prompting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:53.440
all automatically with Weights and Biases?"
"Cool. And is there any other tool on Weights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:59.200
and Biases that you have that you are proud of,&nbsp;
or that - like what else does the platform offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.360
if anything? I mean, that in itself&nbsp;
is already like totally so much."&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.760
"Well, I'd say there's two things we feel&nbsp;
proud of. One is kind of we have a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.640
what we call ML lifecycle tooling. So this is not&nbsp;
just keeping track of the experiments but keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.440
track of okay, what models go into production?&nbsp;
What datasets am I using? So that's called data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.440
lineage. So there's lifecycle things. But I think&nbsp;
for you and your classes, the thing that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:29.280
be most compelling is a product we launched a&nbsp;
few months ago called Weave. This is all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.400
visualizing what's happening with your LLMs. This&nbsp;
is really designed for software engineers to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:41.240
what's going on, because we're noticing like&nbsp;
sometimes you do even a simple RAG workflow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.360
it's hard to kind of dig in and see exactly&nbsp;
okay, what were the calls, what exactly was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.120
the prompt? You start adding templates and&nbsp;
these layers of abstraction, and we wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.760
to make it really simple to just kind of dive in&nbsp;
and see okay, every step, exactly what happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.320
how much did it cost, how many tokens was it."
"Yeah. When you say every step, what are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.960
you referring to? I'm assuming you're not&nbsp;
referring to like model interpretability."&nbsp;

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:04.520
"No, sorry. Every step in a workflow&nbsp;
that involves LLM. So like in RAG,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:08.640
it might be searching your vector database,&nbsp;
and then you pull back some results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.960
and then you look what were those results."
"That's amazing, visualizing that. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.680
wow. Wow. Okay, so that is called&nbsp;
Weave. That was just launched. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:23.520
then you also have the - what was that? What&nbsp;
did you say before? Does that have a name?"&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.640
"We call it Models."
"Models, okay. Yeah, wow. Fantastic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:34.280
So tell me more about Weave and your inspiration&nbsp;
for it, and also just what it is as a product."&nbsp;

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:39.320
"Yeah, so you know, we've been helping lots of&nbsp;
companies like Anthropic and OpenAI develop LLMs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:42.880
and that's something we're really comfortable&nbsp;
doing because that's our background as ML&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:49.400
engineers. But we realized the most exciting&nbsp;
applications right now are software developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.720
that are using these LLMs, and they're not always&nbsp;
familiar. Even fine-tuning is a little bit of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.720 --> 00:06:00.080
stretch. I mean, you teach it, but it feels a&nbsp;
little complicated, I think. A common pattern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:06.640
is to just call the LLMs. But then we realized&nbsp;
there's not a lot of tools for them out there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.680
If you want to see exactly how your model made&nbsp;
calls, or if you want to do evaluations for those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.760
LLMs - like you want to know how many times&nbsp;
did I get back the right answer from an LLM,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:22.080
or compare two LLMs, or compare two prompts. So we&nbsp;
realized, okay, we want to have a separate product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.440
that's really for the software developers, and&nbsp;
it's really easy to use, natural for a software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.320
developer to experience because they're a little&nbsp;
different than ML engineers in terms of comfort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:36.360
with statistics maybe, and kind of what they&nbsp;
know and don't know. So yeah, that's why we built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:41.000
Weave, and it's our newest product, so we're extra&nbsp;
excited about it. We'd love for people to try it."&nbsp;

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:46.880
"Yeah, I'm sure that the people that I work&nbsp;
with would be so interested in using this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:52.400
because they want to use LLMs. They're open to&nbsp;
learn some statistics, but it's mainly about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:07:00.160
using the models. I get asked a lot, 'How do&nbsp;
I evaluate my large language model? How do I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.720
compare them?' Obviously, there are these&nbsp;
benchmarks, but it's really cool that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:11.160
actually allow this to be part of the tool and&nbsp;
to let users see if you're using RAG (Retrieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:18.120
Augmented Generation) and you give it a dataset&nbsp;
or a PDF or whatever that you want it to refer to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:26.840
you're able to see the process and help debug or&nbsp;
help improve it. So that's a really amazing tool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.960
I want to use that tool."
"Demo?"&nbsp;

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.760
"Yeah, thank you."
"Yeah, please. I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:41.680
be honored to get a demo. Wow. How do you see&nbsp;
Weave iterating over the next period of time?"&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.440
"Well, you know, it's a new product, so a lot of&nbsp;
it is kind of in conversation with our customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:51.560
I think there's new kinds of evaluations that&nbsp;
people want to do with LLMs that we really want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:56.480
to support. I also think that agents have&nbsp;
made workflows more and more complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:03.240
so we're thinking how do you visualize a workflow&nbsp;
where there might be like a hundred calls to LLM?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:08.760
That's what's top of mind. But if your audience&nbsp;
tries it and takes it in a different direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.400
we'd be excited about that also."
"Yeah. I mean, would you ever be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:17.560
interested in doing Weave for computer&nbsp;
vision models or like expanding the scope?"&nbsp;

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:21.400
"Totally. I mean, I should say it does kind of&nbsp;
work for computer vision models. We've always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:27.760
been sort of application agnostic and tried to get&nbsp;
the right visualizations for what's needed. So you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:34.280
can show pictures, things in Weave. You can't show&nbsp;
like molecules yet, but if some chemists start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:39.360
use it, we'll certainly add a molecule viewer."
"That's cool. And so how would that look in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.960
of the evaluation for a computer vision model?&nbsp;
Like how would that appear on Weights and Biases?"&nbsp;

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:48.000
"Well, you know, I think like exactly the&nbsp;
way to evaluate a vision model depends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.240
on what you're doing. So like bounding boxes&nbsp;
have standard metrics, semantic segmentation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.640
has different standard metrics. So we try&nbsp;
to make the standard things easy to do."&nbsp;

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.440
"So you have that already?"
"We do, yeah."&nbsp;

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:20.560
"Amazing. What do you think are the biggest&nbsp;
opportunities in the next five to 10 years?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:26.680
And then we can move on to what are some of&nbsp;
the biggest potential drawbacks, risk points?"&nbsp;

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:32.040
"You know, biggest opportunities for companies&nbsp;
or students or honestly anything, like whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:39.480
you're drawn to or whatever you're excited about.&nbsp;
It doesn't have to be the biggest, but yeah."&nbsp;

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:47.520
"Biggest... I guess what's exciting to me right&nbsp;
now in this moment is that suddenly AI works for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:52.320
so many tangible things. Like every company&nbsp;
has different things they could do with AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.400
and a lot of them are kind of in the details of&nbsp;
how the company operates. Like a tire company is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:02.520
figuring out better chemistry to make better&nbsp;
tires, or pharma companies figuring out ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:10.240
to test their drugs before they put them in&nbsp;
lab animals, or companies using LLMs to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:16.960
regulatory compliance. I really love all these&nbsp;
ways that these models are tangible and really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:23.520
help people do things. I think the spectrum&nbsp;
of possible applications has just massively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:27.720
broadened in the last two or three years. So&nbsp;
I think there's just an unbelievable amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:35.640
opportunity in almost anything that people do to&nbsp;
apply LLMs at least partially to that problem."&nbsp;

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:40.760
"It is wild how AI is - I mean, for&nbsp;
those of us who were in AI for so long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:45.880
we always said this like, 'Oh, it'll apply to&nbsp;
every industry.' The fact that now your tires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:50.440
are being - tire chemistry is being altered&nbsp;
to help tires, it's just - it's so exciting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:56.080
It really is going into every single industry&nbsp;
now, and it's an exciting time. And thank you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:00.360
for your tools. They've been so helpful."
"Totally. What advice would you give,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:07.720
for example, a student in AI who is relatively&nbsp;
new and doesn't have any background and doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:13.440
know how to get into the field? Maybe a student&nbsp;
or even someone who's graduated and feels like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.880
'Oh, I missed the train, I'm not technical.'&nbsp;
Do you have any advice for those people?"&nbsp;

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:24.920
"Well, I think the feeling that you missed the&nbsp;
train is a sad thing, and I even felt that way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:28.520
I think like six or seven years ago when I&nbsp;
started Weights and Biases, because it was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:34.240
'Man, I've been this executive for 10 years. I&nbsp;
haven't been doing any kind of machine learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:37.600
I'm so out of date. I don't understand any of&nbsp;
this stuff.' So I think if people feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:41.880
they've missed the train, you're not alone. I&nbsp;
think everyone is feeling FOMO because there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:49.400
so much opportunity right now. You haven't missed&nbsp;
the train. I mean, it's like you can always build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:55.960
technical skills, and I think that it's never been&nbsp;
easier to build technical skills. I mean, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.960 --> 00:12:02.000
so many free resources. But I worry, you know,&nbsp;
with all the free kind of teaching resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:08.480
the way I learn is always kind of through&nbsp;
playing and tinkering and building small things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:14.240
that I actually care about. That's my method.&nbsp;
I think other people learn in different ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:19.200
but I would at least encourage people to&nbsp;
try to make an end-to-end thing that works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:23.320
that satisfies something. Like the other day,&nbsp;
I was building a little website where you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:27.440
kind of put in your text, and then it would&nbsp;
make the text conform to my personal opinion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:31.640
about good style, good writing style. I&nbsp;
was trying to give it to my team to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:34.720
'Hey, why don't you, before you send&nbsp;
me an email, run it through this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:39.040
and then I will love to read your email.'"
"So how did you - how did you do that?"&nbsp;

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:43.120
"Well, I made it - I made it with Vercel, which&nbsp;
is actually a lot of fun. It's like pretty simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:47.440
you know, and these APIs are not hard to call.&nbsp;
So it was actually pretty - I felt like it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:52.640
a little bit of a beginner project. I mean,&nbsp;
I'm not the best - I'm not the best engineer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:56.920
so it was like a - it was a web tool where&nbsp;
people could run it through, run their email&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.920 --> 00:13:00.440
through and get a critique and see if -"
"Yeah, and how did you end up training that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:04.720
model? So you - I'm assuming you didn't train the&nbsp;
model. You just passed in prompts, or how did you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:08.640
go about this end-to-end project just for our -"
"Yeah, so I'd written a style guide for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:11.280
company to say here's how I think&nbsp;
I would like people to write."&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:13.680
"Okay, yeah."
"And so I didn't do any training. I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:19.400
literally made a prompt that I said, 'Hey, OpenAI,&nbsp;
put the next thing in this style guide.' You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:22.720
and it was funny because I was like running&nbsp;
all these emails that I was getting through it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:27.040
and I was like, 'Wow, I actually really prefer&nbsp;
the style guide that I'm telling people.'"&nbsp;

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:29.280
"So it's actually working."
"That's so cool."&nbsp;

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:32.920
"Yeah, it was a really fun little project. But I&nbsp;
think there's like millions of projects like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:38.160
and at least for me, I think that's the best way&nbsp;
for me to learn. So I would recommend that to at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:42.920
least try building some kind of fun end-to-end&nbsp;
app that's tangible that you can show people."&nbsp;

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:49.040
"Yeah, right. And for those of you who don't&nbsp;
know, Vercel is a web app deployment company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:52.920
They perhaps do other things, but that's what&nbsp;
I know them as. And that's - yeah, my website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:57.160
is hosted on Vercel. That's correct. I'm - I'm&nbsp;
correct on - that's how you used it as well?"&nbsp;

00:13:57.160 --> 00:14:00.720
"That's how - yeah, yeah, exactly."
"Exactly. Yeah. I have received this question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:10.000
from some of the viewers: as a non-technical&nbsp;
person coming into AI, what makes - what means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:15.200
they've made it? Like what do they need to do to&nbsp;
feel like they're actually worthy to be there and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:21.000
like they've made it and they can be in the room?"
"Well, hopefully, everyone can be in the room."&nbsp;

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:25.440
"Of course. I feel the same way. Everyone could&nbsp;
be in the room, but but they have this, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:30.960
like, 'Oh, I don't understand how AI works. I need&nbsp;
to - I don't have a background in AI. How do I get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:36.480
into the field? How do I -' I guess even - even&nbsp;
