00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:09.920
some classes.' Like even our chief people officer&nbsp;
did a really cool project using GPT that I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:19.560
absolutely loved, where she was kind of helping do&nbsp;
self-assessments with GPT. So I think using these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:25.160
models in different ways that you can talk about&nbsp;
really shows a level of curiosity. And honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:30.680
no one has more than two years of experience with&nbsp;
LLMs right now, so it's kind of the easiest place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:38.680
to catch up. So I think if you feel kind of left&nbsp;
behind or like you don't have a place, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:42.240
definitely fixable. I'm probably the wrong person&nbsp;
to talk about that because I went to Stanford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:46.440
and I've been doing this for like 20 years, but&nbsp;
honestly, I really don't think - I think the vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:51.160
majority of my experience is honestly - I have&nbsp;
to throw it away, because you have to have like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:57.080
a beginner mind with stuff. I worked really hard&nbsp;
on translation, machine translation. I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.080 --> 00:16:01.040
I have these papers that I wrote in machine&nbsp;
translation, and they're completely worthless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:05.200
It's like that was a dead end. LLMs just do&nbsp;
it without any knowledge of translation, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:09.600
you just have to accept okay, they do it better,&nbsp;
and let's move on and learn about the new thing."&nbsp;

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:13.920
"Right, and that putting your brain to work&nbsp;
in that way does kind of train your brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:17.200
to think in a certain way. So it's not&nbsp;
like the learning is lost. It's not like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:19.800
it was all for naught, you know."
"That's a nice way of putting it."&nbsp;

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:26.320
"Yeah. Right. Like you - you get that practice.&nbsp;
But that is really great. And so you hire quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:33.080
a lot of people that don't have a classical AI&nbsp;
background, but they are showing interest, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:37.440
learning on their own, maybe do a small project&nbsp;
like the prompting project, like a fine-tuning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:42.840
project, like a basic coding implementation.&nbsp;
So are those the kinds of questions you ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:47.480
when you're interviewing? And this might be&nbsp;
secret, but if you are willing to share -"&nbsp;

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:53.040
"No, totally. It's funny because I say this like&nbsp;
publicly, but people don't do it. So, you know, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:57.160
lot of people show up - if you're going to show up&nbsp;
for non-technical roles at Weights and Biases, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:01.320
I'd say about half of our company, a little less&nbsp;
